1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,763
<font color="#FFFFFF">Infinite-width theory, built on tools like the
Neural Tangent Kernel,</font>

2
00:00:04,843 --> 00:00:09,483
<font color="#FFFFFF">has transformed how we understand deep network
initialisation and training,</font>

3
00:00:09,563 --> 00:00:12,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it has only ever described a network's output.</font>

4
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:17,036
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper extends that theory to the network's
input-output Jacobian.</font>

5
00:00:17,116 --> 00:00:24,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors show that a multilayer perceptron and
its Jacobian jointly converge to a Gaussian process as width grows,</font>

6
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:28,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">define a Jacobian Neural Tangent Kernel that
governs training,</font>

7
00:00:28,383 --> 00:00:33,275
<font color="#FFFFFF">and prove that training with a Jacobian
regulariser, so-called robust training,</font>

8
00:00:33,355 --> 00:00:37,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">behaves in the infinite-width limit like a simple
kernel regression.</font>

9
00:00:37,635 --> 00:00:41,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">Along the way they explain, for the first time from
this angle,</font>

10
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:45,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">why Jacobian regularisation makes networks both
accurate and robust.</font>

11
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:47,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">Over the last few years,</font>

12
00:00:47,732 --> 00:00:52,823
<font color="#FFFFFF">infinite-width theory has given us remarkably clean
answers about deep networks.</font>

13
00:00:52,903 --> 00:00:57,413
<font color="#FFFFFF">Tools like the Neural Tangent Kernel tell us how a
network initialises,</font>

14
00:00:57,493 --> 00:01:00,645
<font color="#FFFFFF">how it trains, and where gradient descent ends up.</font>

15
00:01:00,725 --> 00:01:04,718
<font color="#FFFFFF">But there is a catch: all of this describes a
network's output.</font>

16
00:01:04,798 --> 00:01:08,468
<font color="#FFFFFF">It says nothing about the network's input-output Jacobian,</font>

17
00:01:08,548 --> 00:01:11,765
<font color="#FFFFFF">the object that captures how smooth the network is.</font>

18
00:01:11,845 --> 00:01:18,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">And smoothness is exactly what we care about when
we want robustness to noise or to adversarial attacks.</font>

19
00:01:18,568 --> 00:01:24,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">So a natural question is left wide open: can we
extend infinite-width theory to the Jacobian itself,</font>

20
00:01:25,032 --> 00:01:28,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">and to training that deliberately regularises it?</font>

21
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:32,668
<font color="#FFFFFF">Jacobian regularisation is one of those methods
that just works.</font>

22
00:01:32,748 --> 00:01:37,579
<font color="#FFFFFF">Add a penalty on the network's input-output
Jacobian and you get a simple,</font>

23
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:40,499
<font color="#FFFFFF">strong defence against adversarial examples.</font>

24
00:01:40,579 --> 00:01:43,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">The problem is that nobody could really explain why.</font>

25
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:49,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">The theory that had cracked open ordinary training,
the infinite-width limit,</font>

26
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:54,502
<font color="#FFFFFF">had never been pushed to cover the Jacobian, let
alone training that penalises it.</font>

27
00:01:54,582 --> 00:02:00,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper asks whether that same lens can finally
explain what Jacobian regularisation is doing.</font>

28
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:04,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes four moves.</font>

29
00:02:04,692 --> 00:02:07,984
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

30
00:02:08,064 --> 00:02:11,356
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proves that at initialisation a multilayer
perceptron and its Jacobian jointly converge</font>

31
00:02:11,436 --> 00:02:14,729
<font color="#FFFFFF">to a zero-mean Gaussian process,</font>

32
00:02:14,809 --> 00:02:18,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it writes down that limiting kernel, the
Jacobian NNGP kernel,</font>

33
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:21,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">inductively over depth.</font>

34
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:24,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it defines a Jacobian Neural Tangent
Kernel, the JNTK,</font>

35
00:02:24,925 --> 00:02:28,217
<font color="#FFFFFF">and proves it becomes deterministic at
initialisation and then stays constant throughout</font>

36
00:02:28,297 --> 00:02:31,589
<font color="#FFFFFF">robust training as width grows.</font>

37
00:02:31,669 --> 00:02:34,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, because the kernel is constant,</font>

38
00:02:35,041 --> 00:02:38,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">the training dynamics collapse to a linear
first-order differential equation whose infinite-time</font>

39
00:02:38,414 --> 00:02:41,706
<font color="#FFFFFF">solution is a plain kernel regressor.</font>

40
00:02:41,786 --> 00:02:45,078
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth,</font>

41
00:02:45,158 --> 00:02:48,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">it validates all of this empirically and uses the
solution to study when accuracy and robustness go together.</font>

42
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:50,087
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how it works.</font>

43
00:02:50,167 --> 00:02:55,501
<font color="#FFFFFF">Robust training minimises the usual mean-squared
error plus lambda times a penalty on</font>

44
00:02:55,581 --> 00:02:58,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">the network's Jacobian at each training point.</font>

45
00:02:58,510 --> 00:03:02,633
<font color="#FFFFFF">To handle the joint object, the network and its
Jacobian together,</font>

46
00:03:02,713 --> 00:03:08,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors recast the convergence question as a
tensor program and apply the Master theorem.</font>

47
00:03:08,636 --> 00:03:15,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">They then define the finite JNTK from the
parameter-gradients of both the output and each Jacobian component.</font>

48
00:03:15,577 --> 00:03:19,955
<font color="#FFFFFF">The crucial step is showing this kernel does not
move during training;</font>

49
00:03:20,035 --> 00:03:24,349
<font color="#FFFFFF">that turns the learning dynamics into a linear
differential equation,</font>

50
00:03:24,429 --> 00:03:29,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its infinite-time solution is a kernel
regression using the limiting JNTK,</font>

51
00:03:29,397 --> 00:03:34,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">rescaled by a simple diagonal matrix that carries
the regularisation strength lambda.</font>

52
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:38,923
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments are deliberately small and controlled.</font>

53
00:03:39,003 --> 00:03:42,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test the kernel-convergence claims,</font>

54
00:03:42,895 --> 00:03:46,708
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors use a synthetic 256-point dataset in
four dimensions,</font>

55
00:03:46,788 --> 00:03:50,601
<font color="#FFFFFF">built to approximate an even covering of the sphere,</font>

56
00:03:50,681 --> 00:03:54,494
<font color="#FFFFFF">and sweep every combination of width from 64 up to
8192 and depth one, two,</font>

57
00:03:54,574 --> 00:03:58,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">and three.</font>

58
00:03:58,466 --> 00:04:02,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test that the kernel really stays constant
during robust training,</font>

59
00:04:02,359 --> 00:04:06,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">they switch to the Algerian forest fire dataset
from the UCI repository,</font>

60
00:04:06,252 --> 00:04:10,065
<font color="#FFFFFF">224 points with eleven input features,</font>

61
00:04:10,145 --> 00:04:13,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">treated as a plus-or-minus-one regression problem.</font>

62
00:04:14,037 --> 00:04:17,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">The same setups let them line up robust training
against standard training head to head.</font>

63
00:04:18,230 --> 00:04:19,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">The theory holds up.</font>

64
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:21,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">As the network gets wider,</font>

65
00:04:21,334 --> 00:04:26,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">the finite Jacobian NNGP kernel closes in on its
predicted deterministic limit,</font>

66
00:04:26,665 --> 00:04:30,499
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the scaled finite JNTK converges to the limiting JNTK,</font>

67
00:04:30,579 --> 00:04:33,265
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching the two initialisation theorems.</font>

68
00:04:33,345 --> 00:04:35,087
<font color="#FFFFFF">And during robust training,</font>

69
00:04:35,167 --> 00:04:42,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">the gap between the finite JNTK and its limit
shrinks monotonically with width at every training step we checked,</font>

70
00:04:42,792 --> 00:04:46,289
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly what the constancy theorem predicts.</font>

71
00:04:46,369 --> 00:04:51,754
<font color="#FFFFFF">The largest networks tested don't fully nail the
bound because of compute limits,</font>

72
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:57,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the trend is unmistakable: wider is closer, in
precisely the way the theory says.</font>

73
00:04:57,870 --> 00:05:02,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">The most striking finding comes from analysing the
kernel-regression solution.</font>

74
00:05:03,078 --> 00:05:04,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">With Jacobian regularisation,</font>

75
00:05:05,014 --> 00:05:09,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">the eigenfeatures that are more accurate are also
more robust;</font>

76
00:05:09,153 --> 00:05:11,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">accuracy and robustness move together.</font>

77
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:14,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">Standard training shows almost no such link,</font>

78
00:05:14,628 --> 00:05:20,423
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it never produces the accurate-but-fragile
features seen in earlier finite-NTK work.</font>

79
00:05:20,503 --> 00:05:25,163
<font color="#FFFFFF">So the regulariser isn't just trading a little
accuracy for robustness;</font>

80
00:05:25,243 --> 00:05:26,365
<font color="#FFFFFF">it aligns the two.</font>

81
00:05:26,445 --> 00:05:32,307
<font color="#FFFFFF">A separate check on the key full-rank assumption
shows it is fragile: the JNTK's smallest</font>

82
00:05:32,387 --> 00:05:37,648
<font color="#FFFFFF">eigenvalue is far below the NTK's and only becomes
positive for deeper networks,</font>

83
00:05:37,728 --> 00:05:41,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">depth eleven for GeLU but just six for the erf activation.</font>

84
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:44,134
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers anchor the study.</font>

85
00:05:44,214 --> 00:05:48,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">Widths span 64 to 8192, powers of two from six to
thirteen.</font>

86
00:05:48,619 --> 00:05:56,601
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every experiment is repeated ten times and reported
with ninety-five percent bootstrap confidence intervals,</font>

87
00:05:56,681 --> 00:06:01,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the covariance estimates rest on a full million
Monte-Carlo samples.</font>

88
00:06:02,056 --> 00:06:07,649
<font color="#FFFFFF">Robust training uses a Jacobian coefficient of
0.01, a scaling kappa of 0.1,</font>

89
00:06:07,729 --> 00:06:09,665
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a learning rate of one.</font>

90
00:06:09,745 --> 00:06:16,085
<font color="#FFFFFF">The full-rank condition first kicks in at depth
eleven for GeLU and depth six for erf,</font>

91
00:06:16,165 --> 00:06:22,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the theory's width requirement scales like
N-squared times log-N to the twelve-L.</font>

92
00:06:22,810 --> 00:06:27,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">The bottom line is that infinite-width theory now
reaches the Jacobian.</font>

93
00:06:27,202 --> 00:06:31,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">Train a wide network with a Jacobian regulariser
and, in the limit,</font>

94
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:35,721
<font color="#FFFFFF">it behaves like kernel regression with a Jacobian
Neural Tangent Kernel.</font>

95
00:06:35,801 --> 00:06:42,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">And that same regulariser makes accuracy and
robustness pull in the same direction instead of fighting each other,</font>

96
00:06:42,854 --> 00:06:47,599
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving us the first principled explanation, from
the infinite-width viewpoint,</font>

97
00:06:47,679 --> 00:06:52,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">of why penalising the Jacobian yields networks that
are both accurate and robust.</font>
